.TH WPRINTIDLE-C 1 "2026-01-01" "wprintidle-c" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wprintidle-c \- Wayland idle time monitor with signal-based querying
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wprintidle-c
.RI [ timeout_ms ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wprintidle-c
monitors user idle time on Wayland compositors that support the
.B ext-idle-notify-v1
protocol. The program runs continuously in the background and reports idle time
when queried via Unix signals.
.PP
The idle time represents how long the user has been inactive (no keyboard or
mouse activity). When queried, the program prints the current idle duration to
standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I timeout_ms
Time in milliseconds before the user is considered idle. This is the minimum
threshold for idle detection. Default: 1000 (1 second).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Print the current idle time in seconds to standard output.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Print the current idle time in milliseconds to standard output.
.SH USAGE
To start the idle monitor with default settings:
.PP
.nf
.RS
wprintidle-c &
.RE
.fi
.PP
To start with a custom idle timeout of 5 seconds:
.PP
.nf
.RS
wprintidle-c 5000 &
.RE
.fi
.PP
To query the current idle time:
.PP
.nf
.RS
kill -SIGUSR1 $(pidof wprintidle-c)
.RE
.fi
.SH SYSTEMD INTEGRATION
The program can be managed as a systemd user service:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl --user enable wprintidle-c.service
systemctl --user start wprintidle-c.service
.RE
.fi
.PP
Query idle time when running as a service:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl --user kill --signal=SIGUSR1 wprintidle-c.service
.RE
.fi
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B wprintidle-c
requires:
.IP \(bu 2
A Wayland compositor
.IP \(bu 2
Compositor support for the
.B ext-idle-notify-v1
protocol
.PP
The program will exit with an error if these requirements are not met.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful termination
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred (invalid arguments, Wayland connection failed, or protocol not supported)
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Usage
Start the monitor and query idle time every 10 seconds:
.PP
.nf
.RS
wprintidle-c &
PID=$!
while true; do
    kill -SIGUSR1 $PID
    sleep 10
done
.RE
.fi
.SS Screen Locker Integration
Use with a screen locker that activates after 5 minutes of idle time:
.PP
.nf
.RS
wprintidle-c 1000 &
PID=$!
while true; do
    IDLE=$(kill -SIGUSR1 $PID 2>/dev/null | tail -1)
    if [ "$IDLE" -gt 300 ]; then
        swaylock
    fi
    sleep 30
done
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR signal (7)
.PP
Wayland protocol extension:
.I ext-idle-notify-v1
